 {"id":"fabro-5082","title":"Engine: oversized checkpoint event exceeds the events-endpoint body limit (413) and kills the run — two 2h merge-upstream runs destroyed mid-flight","status":"in_progress","type":"bug","priority":0,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T13:19:08.232Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T14:56:35.113Z","description":"WHAT HAPPENED (2026-09-11, two instances): MergeUpstream runs die irrecoverably at their conflict-resolution checkpoint with `worker lost canonical run store during append run event: request failed with status 413 Payload Too Large: Failed to buffer the request body: length limit exceeded` (event=checkpoint.completed). Instance 1: run 01M27V69K6BVS8RNEK3PAAT48Q died 09:29 after 28m (also `Git push failed` immediately before — likely the same oversized-payload class on the push path or a consequence). Instance 2: run 01M2837TP51DNK159MHVPTCVVK died 13:13:36 after 111m of healthy conflict-resolution work (tool calls seconds before death, 0 agent errors) — the accumulated checkpoint payload simply crossed the HTTP body limit. Conductor passes 01M27V3Y12QD and 01M2834T4ZWY routed 'Merge child failed' correctly; ~2.5h of merge work was destroyed. ROOT CAUSE: the worker->server event append (POST /runs/{id}/events, the canonical store) has a request body length limit with no ceiling-aware path for large checkpoint payloads; a run whose serialized state (diff summaries, context, journals of a big merge) grows past it dies at the next checkpoint with no truncation, chunking, or blob offload. KNOWN PRIOR ART: evidence capture already enforces a 16 KB inline ceiling with blob-ref detours elsewhere; the checkpoint event path lacks the equivalent. FIX DIRECTIONS (engine, fabro-server/fabro-workflow): (a) offload oversized event payloads to the blob store and inline a reference (preferred — no data loss); (b) or chunked append; (c) at minimum, fail the checkpoint with a resumable error instead of stopping the workflow run. IMPACT: every large merge (busy fork + high upstream drift) hits this — the merge leg is effectively capped by payload size, not by conflict complexity. Manual /merge-upstream remains the workaround. Basis: runs 01M27V69K6BVS8RNEK3PAAT48Q and 01M2837TP51DNK159MHVPTCVVK (+ conductors 01M27V3Y12QD, 01M2834T4ZWY), server log 13:13:36 UTC, 2026-09-11","labels":["engine","workflows"]}
 {"id":"fabro-a723","title":"checkpoint.completed event exceeds 3MB body limit: 413 kills run AFTER successful merge","status":"closed","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T14:01:43.433Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T14:56:35.025Z","description":"Observed 2026-09-11 on merge-upstream runs 01M27V69 and 01M2837: the worker appends checkpoint.completed whose JSON body exceeds MAX_RUN_EVENT_BODY_BYTES (3MB, fabro-types/src/run_event/mod.rs). Server rejects with 413 Payload Too Large, event sink loses the canonical store, workflow stops, run is marked failed(workflow_error) — even though the merge commit already exists and the gate passed. Result: complete work stranded in an ephemeral sandbox, conductor pass soft-stops, next pass redoes ~2h and ~2 of LLM work.\n\nAgent tool outputs are already bounded (fabro-agent truncation.rs MAX_SERIALIZED_TOOL_OUTPUT_BYTES = limit/2; tool_execution.rs event_body_budget). The workflow-side checkpoint event has NO equivalent bound: whatever it inlines (reports/journal/diff?) can exceed the limit.\n\nFix direction: bound or blob-offload large fields in checkpoint (and any other workflow-emitted) events before POST, mirroring the agent-side truncation contract; add a regression test that builds a >limit checkpoint event and proves the worker truncates instead of losing the store.","closedAt":"2026-09-11T14:56:35.025Z"}
 {"id":"fabro-6beb","title":"fabro/run/* branch pushes rejected by branch protection: finished run work is never persisted remotely","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T14:01:43.531Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T17:36:20.627Z","description":"Observed 2026-09-11: run lifecycle pushes of refs/heads/fabro/run/<id> (and possibly fabro/meta/* in some cases) are rejected by GitHub with 'github rejected the ref - likely a branch protection rule or push ruleset' (runs 01M27V69 11:29, 01M2837 15:13). Consequence: when a sandbox dies or a run fails late, the only copy of finished work is the ephemeral sandbox filesystem; salvage requires docker cp from stopped containers.\n\nFix direction: allow-list fabro/run/* and fabro/meta/* refs in the denkhaus/fabro repo ruleset (ops change), and/or make the engine fall back to a pushable ref namespace on rejection and surface the persistent failure in run notices. Without this, any post-merge infrastructure failure is unrecoverable-by-design.","labels":["needs-user"]}
-{"id":"fabro-7893","title":"Upstream v0.353.0 broke stored-event deserialization: CostSource 'estimated' removed in lithos-llm, run-history activation fails and server crash-loops on upgrade with existing DB","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":0,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T15:42:40.378Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T17:36:20.555Z","description":"Observed 2026-09-11 17:35 Berlin during 'just up' after merging upstream v0.353.0-nightly.0 (PR #124): the new binary's SQLite run-history activation replays every stored event through current types; lithos-llm (pinned rev a1e3fd37) replaced CostSource::Estimated with catalog/provider/application, so legacy events carrying cost_source='estimated' fail with 'Invalid event payload: invalid stored event: unknown variant estimated'. wait-healthy fails and fabro-fabro-1 crash-loops (server down). The dogfood-gate missed this because it runs on a fresh clone with no legacy run history. Fix (landing separately): normalize_legacy_cost_source in fabro-types rewrites 'estimated' to 'catalog' at read time (RunEvent::from_value), which fabro-store uses for stored-event deserialization. Follow-up options: (a) bump lithos-llm if they add a serde alias upstream; (b) extend dogfood-gate with a legacy-DB fixture so upgrades are tested against real historical events.","assignee":"fabro"}
+{"id":"fabro-7893","title":"Upstream v0.353.0 broke stored-event deserialization: CostSource 'estimated' removed in lithos-llm, run-history activation fails and server crash-loops on upgrade with existing DB","status":"closed","type":"bug","priority":0,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T15:42:40.378Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T20:35:21.705Z","description":"Observed 2026-09-11 17:35 Berlin during 'just up' after merging upstream v0.353.0-nightly.0 (PR #124): the new binary's SQLite run-history activation replays every stored event through current types; lithos-llm (pinned rev a1e3fd37) replaced CostSource::Estimated with catalog/provider/application, so legacy events carrying cost_source='estimated' fail with 'Invalid event payload: invalid stored event: unknown variant estimated'. wait-healthy fails and fabro-fabro-1 crash-loops (server down). The dogfood-gate missed this because it runs on a fresh clone with no legacy run history. Fix (landing separately): normalize_legacy_cost_source in fabro-types rewrites 'estimated' to 'catalog' at read time (RunEvent::from_value), which fabro-store uses for stored-event deserialization. Follow-up options: (a) bump lithos-llm if they add a serde alias upstream; (b) extend dogfood-gate with a legacy-DB fixture so upgrades are tested against real historical events.","assignee":"fabro","closedAt":"2026-09-11T20:35:21.705Z","closeReason":"superseded: fix landed in 65d17645 (PR #126) — normalize_legacy_cost_source at read time; follow-ups 7c09f6e5, 33f1ff6a (PR #127) cleared remaining legacy shapes; all 70,628 stored events replay with 0 failures"}
 {"id":"fabro-91bf","title":"Handoff 2026-09-11: conductor outage day - upstream merge salvaged+landed, 413+legacy-event fixes deployed, schedule paused; TODO list inside","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-11T16:40:03.249Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-11T16:40:03.249Z","description":"HANDOFF 2026-09-11 (Session \"Conductor-Outage-Tag\", Agent-Monitoring + Fixes). Alles in Europe/Berlin.\n\n== TAGESVERLAUF (Kurzform) ==\n- 11:00 Conductor-Pass feuerte pünktlich nach just up + Aktivierung. Merge-Child 01M27V6 lief gesund bis 11:27, dann ZAI-Usage-Limit (429, 5h-Fenster, Reset 13:14:50 Berlin als Peking-Zeit). Run failed(workflow_error), Pass soft_stop; 12:00/12:30-Passes starben am selben Limit; User pausierte Schedule.\n- 13:20 Revival per manuellem API-Trigger (POST /api/v1/automations/conductor/runs): Pass 01M2834 + Merge-Child 01M2837. Gate-Triage: alle Test-Failures vorbestehende Flakes (run_wait.rs pre-existing at HEAD, ask_fabro both green).\n- 15:13 Merge fertig (Commit f95a8e019, two-parent 702c657b4+290e0d7e6, v0.353.0-nightly.0), aber Run starb am 413-Bug (checkpoint.completed > 3MB -> Event-Persistenz weg). Salvage: gestoppten Sandbox-Container gestartet, .git per docker cp gezogen, Branch salvage/merge-upstream-01M2837 gepusht (fabro/run/* ist von Branch-Protection geblockt!).\n\n== ERLEDIGT ==\n1) PR #124 MERGED: Upstream-Merge v0.353.0-nightly.0 auf denkhaus (aus dem Salvage-Branch, regular merge).\n2) PR #125 MERGED (fabro-5082): run_event::bound begrenzt jedes kanonische RunEvent auf 3MB-256KB (diff Head/Tail 512KB, Context/Outcome-Marker, Map-Wholesale-Ersatz, generischer String-Pass, run.notice(event_body_overflow) als letzte Instanz); Hook in to_run_event_at + tracing::warn.\n3) PR #126 MERGED (fabro-7893 Teil 1): Legacy cost_source estimated -> catalog (normalize_legacy_cost_source, rekursiv).\n4) PR #127 OPEN (fabro-7893 Teil 2): usage-Keys wie billing im Wrapper-Normalizer + model-lose Wrapper -> null. VERIFIZIERT gegen ALLE 70.628 gespeicherten Events (0 Failures). WICHTIG: Der laufende Server (Image 18:34) enthält diesen Fix bereits (just up lief aus dem Fix-Branch). ACHTUNG: denkhaus hat inzwischen 7c09f6e5b (weiterer lithos-Billing-Wrapper-Normalizer vom autonomous line) - PR #127 beim Merge auf Konflikte pruefen, ggf. als dupliziert schliessen.\n5) Server seit 18:34 healthy; Run-History-Aktivierung validiert alle 70.628 Events; Slack + UI ok.\n6) Seeds: fabro-5082 (P0, in_progress), fabro-7893 (P0, in_progress), fabro-6beb (P1, open), fabro-a723 (closed als Duplikat von 5082). Mulch: engine-failure-Record zum 413+Salvage-Verfahren.\n7) Monitoring-Heartbeats: alle geloescht (conductor-watch, fix-rollout).\n\n== AKUELLER ZUSTAND ==\n- Server: faehrt, /health ok, Image von 18:34 (enthält 125+126+127-Inhalt).\n- Conductor-Automation: Schedule VOM USER PAUSIERT (enabled=false, expression */30 * * * *, breaker leer, kein last_error). Manueller api-Trigger bleibt enabled.\n- Lokales Repo: auf denkhaus @ 7c09f6e5b. Branches fix/run-event-body-budget + fix/legacy-usage-wrapper + salvage/merge-upstream-01M2837 existieren remote (PRs 125/126 gemerged, 127 offen).\n- dev token: ~/.fabro/auth.json; API http://localhost:32276; PUT auf automations braucht If-Match: <ETag aus GET>.\n\n== NOCH ZU TUN (Reihenfolge empfohlen) ==\n1) PR #127: Gate abwarten, Merge-Konflikt gegen 7c09f6e5b pruefen (Inhalt teils ueberlappend), dann lokales Repo aufraeumen (fix-Branches loeschen, denkhaus pullen, einmal just up damit Binary == denkhaus-HEAD).\n2) Conductor-Schedule reaktivieren, wenn Betrieb gewuenscht: GET /api/v1/automations -> triggers[schedule].enabled=true -> PUT mit If-Match. Naechste Fire dann :00/:30.\n3) fabro-6beb: Branch-Protection/Ruleset fabro/run/* und fabro/meta/* freigeben (oder Engine-Fallback auf pushbaren Ref-Namespace). Ohne das bleibt fertige Run-Arbeit nur im Sandbox-FS (heutiger Salvage-Aufwand war nur manuell moeglich).\n4) fabro-5082 Follow-up: Blob-Offload statt Truncation (Richtung a im Seed).\n5) fabro-7893 Follow-up: dogfood-gate mit Legacy-DB-Fixture (Upgrade-Pfade mit historischen Events testen; heute habe der Gate beide Breaks nicht gesehen).\n6) Nebenfunde: (a) Sandbox-Container reappt Zombies nicht (PID 1 sleep infinity); (b) Container fabro-run-01M2808 lief nach Terminal-Failure 6h weiter - Cleanup nach failed runs pruefen; (c) nextest-Integrationstests timeouten unter Volllast (-j 4 hilft); (d) ZAI-Stoerungen: 5h-Usage-Limit + wiederkehrende Internal network failure + ein ~10min haengender Request - Retry-Politik bei Netzwerk-Haengern pruefen (fabro-llm).\n7) Diagnose-Artefakte koennen weg: /tmp/all-events.jsonl, /tmp/fabro-copy.sqlite3, /tmp/just-up-126.log, /tmp/just-up-127.log (falls existiert).\n8) Validierungs-Trick fuer später: gespeicherte Events als JSONL dumpen und via RunEvent::from_value replayen (temp. ignored test) - findet Legacy-Breaks vor dem Deploy.\n\nMonitoring bei Bedarf neu aufsetzen: rlm_heartbeat.create(...) + api()-Helper mit dev token; Zeiten immer Europe/Berlin ausgeben (User-Vorgabe)."}
